 # Out-of-core Interval Tree Extraction
-
-
+Also called: Chiang, Silva, and Schroeder method, Out-of-Core Interval Tree
 ## Overview
-Out-of-core Interval Tree Extraction is a method for isosurface extraction that stores the interval tree and associated min/max lists on disk in blocks, loading only the necessary blocks based on isovalue intervals [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Standard Interval Tree for Isosurface Extraction](1230-standard-interval-tree-for-isosurface-extraction.md) by partitioning the tree structure to fit within fixed-size disk blocks, enabling the processing of datasets larger than available main memory. Unlike its siblings, it explicitly models the I/O cost of reading tree nodes and min/max lists as a function of isovalue probability distributions [S1, unofficial].
-
